  <p>
    An event that arrives with no round open opens one with <code>spin: false</code>,
    which is how a nudge before any spin still gets measured. The options
    are <code>now</code> (default <code>performance.now</code>), <code>history</code> (20
    rounds kept; <code>clear()</code> empties them but keeps the
    counter), <code>ticker</code> (enables speed sampling)
    and <code>sampleMs</code> (33); <code>MAX_SAMPLES</code> caps speed at 1800 samples
    per reel per round, about 30 seconds. The getters
    are <code>current</code>, <code>rounds</code>, <code>isActive</code> (a reel has an
    open phase, or a spin round has not completed while the set is
    spinning), <code>length</code> (a live clock while active,
    else <code>lastActivityAt</code>)
    and <code>totals {'{ rounds, skipped, averageDuration | null }'}</code>,
    where <code>rounds</code> counts every round ever opened and the other two are over
    the kept window. <code>snapshot()</code> returns a deep JSON copy
    as <code>SpinMetricsSnapshot {'{ rounds, totals }'}</code>. Like the debug snapshot,
    it is not semver-protected.
  </p>

  <h2>Notices</h2>

  <p>
    The library sometimes needs to tell a developer something they should act on: a
    buffer that had to be clamped, a mask that cannot be rounded, a phase chain that
    threw and was recovered by a slam. All of that goes through one
    channel, <code>utils/notify.ts</code>, rather than
    scattered <code>console.warn</code> calls, so you can turn it down, listen to it, or
    route it somewhere. A notice is <code>{'{ kind, code, message, detail }'}</code>,
    and the <code>code</code> is a stable string you can grep for.
  </p>

  <p>
    The level, <code>'silent' | 'error' | 'warn' | 'info'</code>, ranked 0 to 3, gates
    console output only; it defaults to <code>'info'</code> because the messages it
    replaced were unconditional,
    and <code>setLogLevel()</code> and <code>getLogLevel()</code> change and read
    it. <code>onNotice(listener)</code> returns an unsubscribe and hears every notice
    regardless of level, even at <code>'silent'</code>, which is how a test asserts that
    a warning fired without cluttering its
    output. <code>noticeWarnOnce</code> deduplicates by code in a process-wide set and
    skips the listeners on repeats too; <code>resetNoticesForTest()</code> clears both
    the set and the listeners. Output goes through <code>console[kind]</code> so
    devtools filtering and stack traces keep working: a styled badge in a
    browser, <code>[pixi-reels] kind(code) message</code> in Node. Notices are for
    things a developer should act on, never for per-frame state. This is every code the
    library raises today, and where.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60"><tr><th class={th}>Kind</th><th class={th}>Code</th><th class={th}>Raised in</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {NOTICES.map(([kind, code, where]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border/60 align-top">
              <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">{kind}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-primary text-xs">{code}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs text-foreground/80">{where}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Tickers, disposal and gsap</h2>

  <p>
    Three pieces of plumbing underpin everything above, and they are the first things to
    check when a debug tool, or the game, misbehaves: how objects clean up, which gsap
    instance the engine is tweening on, and who is driving gsap's clock.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-3 md:grid-cols-3">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mb-1">Disposable and TickerRef (ADR 004)</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-foreground/80"><code>Disposable</code> is <code>{'{ destroy(): void; readonly isDestroyed }'}</code>. Any class that subscribes to tickers, creates display objects or holds references implements it. The original library's main leak was <code>ticker.add</code> without a matching <code>remove</code>, so no class may call <code>ticker.add()</code> directly: it goes through <code>TickerRef</code>, which keeps its own callback list, ignores <code>add</code> after destroy, only removes callbacks it tracks, and on an idempotent <code>destroy()</code> removes every tracked callback. <code>ReelSet.destroy()</code> cascades through every subsystem; <code>SpinMetrics</code> and the overlay both use <code>TickerRef</code>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mb-1">gsap injection</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-foreground/80">With symlinked workspaces (vite with a linked package, pnpm, esbuild chains) the library's <code>gsap</code> import and yours can resolve to different module instances, each with its own root timeline; the engine's tweens then sit on a timeline nobody drives and stall at progress 0. <code>ReelSetBuilder.gsap(myGsap)</code> hands the engine your instance. It is held per reel set (carried down through <code>ReelConfig.gsap</code>), so composed stages can use different instances. <code>DEFAULT_GSAP</code> is the instance resolved when the library loaded.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mb-1">driveGsapWithTicker</div>
      <p class="text-sm text-foreground/80"><code>driveGsapWithTicker(ticker, instance = DEFAULT_GSAP) → dispose</code> removes <code>gsap.updateRoot</code> from gsap's own ticker and calls it from a Pixi ticker callback with <code>ticker.lastTime / 1000</code>. Why: gsap's requestAnimationFrame loop is throttled or frozen in hidden tabs and iframes (casino lobbies) while the Pixi ticker keeps going, so easing and bounces would freeze mid-flight. Call it once per app, with the same instance you gave the builder; driving from two sources runs animations at double speed. The disposer is idempotent and re-adds <code>updateRoot</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

